gm-method: when a user asks to replace custom code with a library to 'reduce maintenance', check the library's HARD architectural requirements against the project's hardest existing constraint FIRST (e.g. a library assuming a real Node process, when the target project has an in-Worker/sandboxed execution mode with no such runtime) before evaluating feature parity -- a hard incompatibility there ends the full-replacement question immediately, regardless of how good the library otherwise is. THEN check whether the project already has a clean abstraction boundary insulating consumers from the internals being replaced (e.g. an AppContext/facade layer) -- if yes, a PARTIAL swap underneath that boundary is low-risk and worth recording as a future option even when a full swap is declined. Verify any 'standalone usable without the framework' claim about a sub-library (e.g. a schema/serialization piece of a larger framework) via a REAL live install+round-trip trial in a scratch dir, not documentation-reading alone -- installed-package-count (e.g. '2 packages added, zero framework core dragged in') and an actual encode/decode/delta round-trip are the decisive evidence, and decorator-based APIs commonly advertised in docs may not run in plain Node without a build transform, while the SAME library often ships a non-decorator imperative API (e.g. defineTypes-style) that does.